      {# ── THE PRÉCIS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         The philosophy in about four hundred words, rendered from the same source
         as /philosophy so the two cannot quote different figures. Unnumbered: it
         is a preamble to the method, not the first step of it. #}
      <section class="ar-sec" id="why" data-ag-reveal>
        <h3>Why this exists</h3>
        {{ art.blocks(doc_summary) }}
        {{ art.next({
          url: '/philosophy',
          kicker: 'The argument in full',
          title: 'The philosophy behind Africa GATES community voting',
          blurb: 'Sixteen sections on what a vote means here, what we mean by a community, what
                  standing for someone asks of a nominee, and what this model cannot do. Available
                  to copy, download and cite.'
        }) }}
      </section>

      {# ── THE SCORE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
      <section class="ar-sec" id="score" data-ag-reveal>
        <h3><span class="ar-sn">01</span>The score</h3>
        <p>The Cultural Power Index is a weighted combination of the community’s verified votes
           and an independent judge panel. Neither alone decides a winner, and that is the point
           of having both.</p>

        <div class="ar-tw">
          <table class="ar-table">
            <caption>Composition of the Cultural Power Index</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Input</th><th scope="col">Share</th><th scope="col">What it measures</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Community votes</th>
                <td class="ar-num">{{ community_pct|default(45) }}%</td>
                <td>Normalised <strong>within each category</strong>, against that category’s strongest
                    vote count — so a nominee in a small field is not punished for being in one. Only
                    organic, code-verified votes count here; contribution bonus votes never enter
                    this number.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Judge panel</th>
                <td class="ar-num">{{ judge_pct|default(55) }}%</td>
                <td>An independent panel scores each shortlisted nominee on four equally-weighted
                    criteria. Only complete scorecards are counted, and judges do not see the vote
                    tally while scoring.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
            <tfoot>
              <tr><td colspan="3">{{ community_pct|default(45) }}% community + {{ judge_pct|default(55) }}% judges = CPI.
                  The split is a per-cycle setting, and this page always shows the one currently in force.</td></tr>
            </tfoot>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p><strong>Why {{ community_pct|default(45) }}/{{ judge_pct|default(55) }}?</strong> Popularity alone
           should not decide a winner — a well-funded campaign can generate disproportionate vote
           volume, and reach on the internet is not the same thing as impact on the continent. The
           slight tilt to the panel keeps merit, not marketing, in charge of the decision.</p>

        <div class="ar-refs">
          <span>Further reading</span>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/help/how-cpi-works">How the CPI is calculated</a> — the full method, end to end.</li>
            <li><a href="/help/why-a-small-category-is-not-a-disadvantage">Why a small category is not a disadvantage</a> — what normalising inside a category actually does.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      {# ── ELIGIBILITY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
      <section class="ar-sec" id="eligible" data-ag-reveal>
        <h3><span class="ar-sn">02</span>Who can actually win</h3>
        <p>Leading the public vote makes a nominee the front-runner. It does not, by itself, make
           them eligible to be declared the winner.</p>
        <p><strong>Complete judge scorecards, minimum {{ min_judges|default(2) }}.</strong> A nominee needs
           at least {{ min_judges|default(2) }} judges to have scored them on every criterion. A
           half-finished card counts as nothing — not as a zero, which would be a silent penalty for a
           judge’s browser crashing. If nobody in a category reaches the quorum, the award is not
           given: an empty result is more honest than one nobody scored.</p>

        <h4 class="ar-h4">Three exclusions that apply before anything is counted</h4>
        <ul class="ar-list">
          <li><strong>Judges recuse themselves.</strong> A judge with any connection to a nominee steps
              out of scoring them, and a judge’s own votes in a category they judge are excluded from
              the community count.</li>
          <li><strong>The shortlist is frozen.</strong> Once a category opens for voting, nobody can be
              added to it. A late entrant would be competing against totals that had a head start, and
              every earlier vote would have been cast on a different ballot.</li>
          <li><strong>Unverified profiles score nothing.</strong> A profile must clear human review
              before any votes or impact points attach to it, so a fabricated nominee cannot accumulate
              a score while waiting to be noticed.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="ar-refs">
          <span>Further reading</span>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/help/why-the-leader-may-not-be-eligible-to-win">Why the vote leader may not be eligible</a> — quorum, complete cards and recusal, in detail.</li>
            <li><a href="/help/what-happens-if-two-nominees-tie">What happens if two nominees tie</a> — the tiebreak is organic votes, never money.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      {# ── CRITERIA ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
      <section class="ar-sec" id="criteria" data-ag-reveal>
        <h3><span class="ar-sn">03</span>What the judges score</h3>
        <p>Every judge scores each nominee from 0 to 10 on four dimensions. The four carry equal
           weight — there is no hidden multiplier making one of them decide the card.</p>

        <div class="ar-tw">
          <table class="ar-table">
            <caption>The judging rubric</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">Weight</th><th scope="col">What it asks</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {% set crit = {
                'Impact':'The measurable difference made for a community, an industry or the continent. What is different because this person did the work?',
                'Originality':'Inventiveness. Has something genuinely new been introduced, or is this an existing thing done competently?',
                'Reach':'Breadth of influence — local, regional, continental, global. Reach is not follower count.',
                'Integrity':'Consistency of values and accountability under pressure, including how a nominee behaves when it costs them something.'
              } %}
              {% for title, desc in crit %}
                <tr><th scope="row">{{ title }}</th><td class="ar-num">25%</td><td>{{ desc }}</td></tr>
              {% endfor %}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="ar-aside">
          <p>Judges see a nominee’s dossier, not their vote count. The public tally is kept off the
             scoring screen deliberately, so it cannot anchor a score before the judge has formed one.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="ar-refs">
          <span>Further reading</span>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/help/what-the-judges-actually-score">What the judges actually score you on</a> — each criterion, and what evidence counts for it.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      {# ── MONEY ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Published because the separation was real in the engine and invisible on
         the site. A promise nobody can see is not a promise anybody can hold us
         to. #}
      <section class="ar-sec" id="money" data-ag-reveal>
        <h3><span class="ar-sn">04</span>What a contribution can — and cannot — do</h3>
        <p>Supporters can contribute to the programme in a nominee’s name, and each contribution
           adds to that nominee’s public tally. The money keeps the awards running — the panel, the
           verification work, the platform itself. It is fair to ask what it actually buys, and the
           honest answer is: visibility, never rank.</p>

        <div class="ar-cols">
          <div class="ar-cols--can">
            <h4>What a contribution does</h4>
            <ul class="ar-list">
              <li>Adds to the nominee’s <strong>public tally</strong> — the number shown on their page and on the leaderboard.</li>
              <li>Puts the supporter’s name on the nominee’s supporters list, if they chose to be named.</li>
              <li>Sets aside a share for the nominee themselves, once they qualify — see <a href="#return">the community return</a>.</li>
              <li>Funds the judging panel, the verification work and running the programme.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="ar-cols--cannot">
            <h4>What it does not do</h4>
            <ul class="ar-list">
              <li>It <strong>does not move the Cultural Power Index</strong>. The {{ community_pct|default(45) }}%
                  community share is normalised over organic, code-verified votes only. Purchased votes
                  live in a separate column the scorer never reads.</li>
              <li>It <strong>does not break a tie</strong>. When two nominees finish level, the tiebreak
                  is organic votes — one verified supporter outranks any number of purchased ones.</li>
              <li>It is <strong>capped</strong>: purchased votes on a nominee cannot exceed
                  {{ paid_cap_pct|default(50) }}% of the organic support they have earned, so money can
                  amplify a real following and cannot manufacture one.</li>
              <li>It <strong>buys no consideration from a judge</strong>, who cannot see it.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>Put plainly: money can make a nominee <em>look</em> more popular. It cannot make them win.
           If that ever stops being true, this page is wrong and we want to hear about it — the
           numbers above are read from the live scoring configuration, not typed in by hand.</p>

        <div class="ar-refs">
          <span>Further reading</span>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/help/what-paid-votes-do">What paid votes actually do</a> — price, ceiling, and what they are excluded from.</li>
            <li><a href="/help/how-free-voting-works">How free voting works</a> — the vote that does move the score.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      {# ── COMMUNITY RETURN ────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
      <section class="ar-sec" id="return" data-ag-reveal>
        <h3><span class="ar-sn">05</span>The community return</h3>
        <p>If money is going to be part of this, the people whose communities raised it should see
           some of it back. A nominee keeps a share of what supporters contribute in their name —
           whether or not they win, because they raised it either way.</p>

        <div class="ar-tw">
          <table class="ar-table">
            <caption>How a nominee qualifies, and what they keep</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Rule</th><th scope="col">Setting</th><th scope="col">What it means</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Qualifying support</th>
                <td class="ar-num">{{ return_threshold|default(250)|number_format }} votes</td>
                <td>A nominee begins earning once they have gathered
                    {{ return_threshold|default(250)|number_format }} votes of qualifying support.
                    Votes are counted, not heads — somebody who bought twenty-five votes did more
                    than somebody who cast one, and a rule that scored them the same would call
                    generosity noise.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">No single pocket</th>
                <td class="ar-num">{{ return_cap_pct|default(10) }}%</td>
                <td>One supporter’s votes count for at most
                    <strong>{{ return_cap_votes|default(25)|number_format }}</strong> toward that
                    threshold, however many they bought. So it takes at least
                    <strong>{{ return_people|default(10) }} different verified people</strong>,
                    whatever anybody is willing to spend.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">The share</th>
                <td class="ar-num">{{ return_pct|default('50') }}%</td>
                <td>From that point on, {{ return_pct|default('50') }}% of each further contribution is
                    set aside for the nominee. Crossing the line does not reach backwards — earlier
                    contributions stay unshared, so nobody is rewarded retroactively by a threshold
                    moving.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>The ceiling is the safeguard, and it is deliberately not a headcount rule. A threshold in
           raw votes is not a rule at all, it is a <em>price</em>: one person could buy the whole thing
           in a single order and everything genuine afterwards would earn. Capping each supporter’s
           contribution to the threshold keeps that impossible while still letting the people who gave
           most count for most. Free votes and contributions are added together per person, so
           splitting an order cannot buy a second allowance.</p>

        <p>It is held as an <em>append-only ledger</em>, not a balance we can quietly edit. Every entry
           records what it was computed from and at what rate; if a contribution is refunded or charged
           back, the share accrued on it is reversed and the reversal stays on the record beside the
           original. Nothing is payable until that cycle has ended and its results have been
           announced — a nominee cannot cash out mid-race, and the platform is not paying out money an
           integrity finding might still reverse.
           {% if not return_on|default(true) %}The share is currently set to 0%, so nothing is accruing
           at all.{% endif %}</p>

        <div class="ar-refs">
          <span>Further reading</span>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/help/the-community-return">The community return, in full</a> — qualifying support, the ceiling, the ledger and payout.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      {# ── HONEST COUNT ────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
      <section class="ar-sec" id="honest" data-ag-reveal>
        <h3><span class="ar-sn">06</span>Keeping the count honest</h3>
        <p>Every vote needs a one-time code sent to a working email address, and every attempt is
           scored for risk from 0 to 100 <em>before</em> anything is written down.</p>

        <div class="ar-tw">
          <table class="ar-table">
            <caption>Risk bands, and what each one does</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Score</th><th scope="col">Band</th><th scope="col">What happens</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="ar-num">0–{{ fraud_monitor|default(30) }}</td><th scope="row">Clear</th><td>Approved as it is cast.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="ar-num">{{ fraud_monitor|default(30) }}–{{ fraud_flag|default(60) }}</td><th scope="row">Monitored</th><td>Counted, and kept under observation.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="ar-num">{{ fraud_flag|default(60) }}–{{ fraud_block|default(80) }}</td><th scope="row">Flagged</th><td>Held for a person to review.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="ar-num">{{ fraud_block|default(80) }}+</td><th scope="row">Blocked</th><td>Refused before the vote exists.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <ul class="ar-list">
          <li><strong>One identity, one vote</strong>, per category. The address is stored only as a
              SHA-256 hash, so we can tell that two votes came from the same person without holding
              the person.</li>
          <li><strong>Disposable mail is refused</strong> at the moment a code is requested — before any
              database write happens at all.</li>
          <li><strong>A total can go down.</strong> Votes later found to be fraudulent are removed, and a
              refunded contribution takes its bonus votes with it. A falling number is usually the
              integrity system working.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="ar-aside">
          <p>Being flagged is not an accusation. A shared office network or a campus wifi produces the
             same pattern a vote farm does, which is exactly why a flag sends a vote to a human rather
             than to the bin.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="ar-refs">
          <span>Further reading</span>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/help/how-we-spot-a-vote-that-is-not-real">How we decide a vote is not real</a> — the signals, the bands, and what to do if yours was refused.</li>
            <li><a href="/help/already-voted">It says I already voted</a> — the commonest reason a real person is turned away.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      {# ── WHEN MONEY GOES WRONG ───────────────────────────────────────── #}
      <section class="ar-sec" id="failures" data-ag-reveal>
        <h3><span class="ar-sn">07</span>When a payment goes wrong</h3>
        <p>A payment passes through a bank and a gateway before it reaches us, and any of those steps
           can fail after a card has been charged. We would rather say so than pretend it never
           happens.</p>
        <ul class="ar-list">
          <li><strong>The votes are still owed to you.</strong> If the ballot was open when you paid, the
              votes are yours even if the confirmation reached us late. We check the time you
              <em>started</em> the payment, not the moment our system happened to notice it.</li>
          <li><strong>If they cannot be delivered, the money goes back.</strong> Where votes genuinely
              cannot be counted — the nominee left the ballot, or the payment confirmed long after
              voting closed — the order is refunded rather than kept. We do not keep money for votes we
              did not deliver.</li>
          <li><strong>Tell us, with your reference.</strong> Every order carries a reference beginning
              <code>AFG-</code>. Give it to the <a href="/support/assistant">assistant</a> and it
              re-checks the payment with the bank directly, adding the votes on the spot if the money
              arrived.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="ar-aside">
          <p>The same honesty applies to our own failures. When our records show a voting code never
             left the building, that vote can be restored after voting closes — proposed by one person,
             approved by a different one, capped in size, and disclosed on the nominee’s page.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="ar-refs">
          <span>Further reading</span>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/help/paid-but-no-votes">I paid but my votes have not appeared</a> — fixable in under a minute, without waiting for anyone.</li>
            <li><a href="/help/refund-when-votes-cannot-count">When do you refund?</a> — the one case that refunds itself, and the rest.</li>
            <li><a href="/help/votes-we-could-not-deliver">Votes that never reached you</a> — how a failed code becomes a restored vote.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      {# ── SEALED ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
      <section class="ar-sec" id="sealed" data-ag-reveal>
        <h3><span class="ar-sn">08</span>Sealing the result</h3>
        <p>A result you have to take on trust is not a result. Every time the standings are computed
           they are sealed — each record carrying a fingerprint of itself and of the record before it,
           all the way back to the first.</p>
        <p>The consequence is the useful part. Editing an old standing — nudging one number in a table
           months later — breaks its fingerprint, and because the next record contains that
           fingerprint, it breaks every record after it. There is no quiet edit available; there is
           only an edit that announces itself.</p>
        <ol class="ar-steps">
          <li>The chain is re-verified automatically every day, link by link.</li>
          <li>A break is not a line in a log somebody might read. It is raised as a failure, to people,
              with the position of the broken link.</li>
          <li>The database refuses to record two standings claiming the same predecessor, so the chain
              cannot be forked into a convenient second history.</li>
          <li>Every recomputation is logged, and an independent reviewer can replay the arithmetic from
              source.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>This protects you from us as much as from anybody else. A platform that can silently rewrite
           its own results has to be taken on trust; one that cannot does not need to be.</p>

        <div class="ar-refs">
          <span>Further reading</span>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/help/how-results-are-sealed">How you can tell a result was not edited</a> — the chain, the daily check, and what a break looks like.</li>
            <li><a href="/help/dispute-a-result">I think a count or a ranking is wrong</a> — ask for an audit; we will show the working.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      {# ── CYCLE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
      <section class="ar-sec" id="cycle" data-ag-reveal>
        <h3><span class="ar-sn">09</span>The cycle, stage by stage</h3>
        <p>A programme moves through six stages in one direction. The stage is not a label on a page —
           the system enforces it, and each transition closes something permanently.</p>
        <ol class="ar-steps">
          {% set steps = [
            ['Nominated','Anyone can enter someone; entries queue for moderation.'],
            ['Verified','The team checks eligibility, accuracy and completeness.'],
            ['Shortlisted','Approved nominees go public — and the list is frozen.'],
            ['Voted','Code-verified, risk-scored votes are collected.'],
            ['Judged','The panel scores the shortlist, without seeing tallies.'],
            ['Sealed','A signed snapshot is taken; results computed and archived.']
          ] %}
          {% for s in steps %}<li><strong>{{ s[0] }}.</strong> {{ s[1] }}</li>{% endfor %}
        </ol>
        <div class="ar-aside">
          <p>Card contribution closes a little earlier than free voting, so payments already in flight
             have time to land before the ballot shuts. Free voting runs to the bell.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="ar-refs">
          <span>Further reading</span>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/help/the-stages-of-an-award-cycle">The stages, and what freezes at each</a> — what each transition closes, and why.</li>
            <li><a href="/help/when-does-voting-close">When does voting close?</a> — deadlines, and the gap before them.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      {# ── DISPUTES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
      <section class="ar-sec" id="disputes" data-ag-reveal>
        <h3><span class="ar-sn">10</span>Disputes and your data</h3>
        <p>If you believe a result was manipulated or a fraud pattern was missed, say so. Every
           recomputation is logged, so a count can be replayed from the underlying votes and the
           working shown to you.</p>
        <p>Raise it at <a href="mailto:integrity@afrovanguard.org.ng">integrity@afrovanguard.org.ng</a>
           or through <a href="/support">Support &amp; appeals</a>. Appeals go to somebody independent
           of the original decision. We will:</p>
        <ol class="ar-steps">
          <li>Acknowledge the complaint within {{ review_sla_hours }} hours.</li>
          <li>Export the relevant vote audit trail — timestamps, hashed identities, risk scores.</li>
          <li>Engage an independent reviewer if the complaint is substantiated.</li>
          <li>Publish a summary of findings if the dispute affected a final result.</li>
        </ol>
        <h4 class="ar-h4">What we store when you vote</h4>
        <p>A SHA-256 hash of your email and IP, a device fingerprint, and the nominee ID and timestamp
           of your vote. We never store your plain-text email, name or personal identifier against a
           vote, and we never sell or monetise voter data. Full details:
           <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a> · <a href="/terms">Terms</a>.</p>

        <div class="ar-refs">
          <span>Further reading</span>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/help/dispute-a-result">I think a count or a ranking is wrong</a> — what to send, and what happens next.</li>
            <li><a href="/help/report-a-profile">How do I report a nominee or profile?</a> — confidentially, and without needing proof.</li>
            <li><a href="/help/what-data-do-you-keep">What data do you keep about me?</a> — held, never held, and your rights.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

